const ER_QUESTIONS=[
 {id:'vectors',topic:'Vectors & equilibrium',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain and apply',q:'Explain how a force at an angle can be resolved into perpendicular components and how components can be used to decide whether an object is in equilibrium.',plan:['Define resolving a vector.','Explain sine/cosine relative to the stated angle.','State the equilibrium condition in perpendicular directions.'],criteria:[['A vector is replaced by perpendicular components with the same combined effect.',[['component','resolve'],['perpendicular','horizontal','vertical']]],['Uses cosine/sine correctly relative to adjacent/opposite sides.',[['cos','cosine'],['sin','sine']]],['Recognises the angle reference must be checked.',[['angle'],['horizontal','vertical','axis','adjacent','opposite']]],['States equilibrium means zero resultant force.',[['equilibrium'],['zero'],['resultant','net']]],['States component sums are zero in each perpendicular direction.',[['sum','balance','equal'],['horizontal','x'],['vertical','y']]],['Connects zero resultant force to zero acceleration/constant velocity.',[['zero acceleration','constant velocity','stationary']]]],model:'Resolving a vector means replacing it with perpendicular components that have the same combined effect. If an angle is measured from the horizontal, the adjacent horizontal component uses cosine and the opposite vertical component uses sine; the angle reference must always be checked. For equilibrium the vector sum of the forces is zero. Therefore the horizontal components sum to zero and the vertical components sum to zero. The resultant force is zero, so acceleration is zero and the object is stationary or moves at constant velocity.'},
 {id:'moments',topic:'Moments & equilibrium',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain',q:'Explain the conditions required for a rigid body to be in equilibrium and how the principle of moments can be used to analyse the forces acting on it.',plan:['State translational equilibrium.','State rotational equilibrium.','Explain perpendicular distance and moments about a pivot.'],criteria:[['Resultant force must be zero.',[['resultant','net'],['force'],['zero','balance']]],['Acceleration is zero if resultant force is zero.',[['acceleration'],['zero']]],['Resultant moment must be zero.',[['moment'],['zero','balance']]],['Clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments.',[['clockwise'],['anticlockwise'],['equal','balance']]],['Moment uses force times perpendicular distance.',[['force'],['perpendicular'],['distance']]],['Moments must be taken about the same chosen pivot.',[['pivot','point'],['same','chosen']]]],model:'For a rigid body in complete equilibrium, both the resultant force and the resultant moment must be zero. Zero resultant force gives zero translational acceleration. Rotational equilibrium requires the total clockwise moment to equal the total anticlockwise moment about the same chosen pivot. A moment is force multiplied by the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force’s line of action.'},
 {id:'graphs',topic:'Motion graphs',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain',q:'Explain how displacement–time, velocity–time and acceleration–time graphs are related. Include the physical meanings of relevant gradients and areas.',plan:['Link displacement–time gradient to velocity.','Link velocity–time gradient and area.','Link acceleration–time area to change in velocity.'],criteria:[['Gradient of displacement–time gives velocity.',[['gradient'],['displacement','position'],['velocity']]],['Changing displacement–time gradient means changing velocity.',[['changing','change'],['gradient'],['velocity']]],['Gradient of velocity–time gives acceleration.',[['gradient'],['velocity'],['acceleration']]],['Area under velocity–time gives displacement.',[['area'],['velocity'],['displacement']]],['Area under acceleration–time gives change in velocity.',[['area'],['acceleration'],['change'],['velocity']]],['Negative graph values depend on the chosen positive direction and do not automatically mean slowing down.',[['negative'],['direction','sign'],['slowing','speed']]]],model:'The gradient of a displacement–time graph is velocity, so a changing gradient shows changing velocity. The gradient of a velocity–time graph is acceleration, while its signed area is displacement. The signed area under an acceleration–time graph gives the change in velocity. Negative values indicate direction relative to the chosen positive direction; they do not automatically mean that an object is slowing down.'},
 {id:'projectiles',topic:'Projectile motion & drag',marks:6,time:10,command:'Explain and compare',q:'Explain the motion of an ideal projectile after launch and then explain how air resistance changes the trajectory compared with the ideal model.',plan:['Separate horizontal and vertical motion.','Describe acceleration and velocity changes.','Explain the effect of drag on both components and symmetry.'],criteria:[['Horizontal and vertical motions are independent components sharing the same time.',[['horizontal'],['vertical'],['time'],['independent','component']]],['With no drag, horizontal acceleration is zero and horizontal velocity is constant.',[['horizontal'],['acceleration'],['zero'],['constant','velocity']]],['Vertical acceleration is g downward throughout the flight.',[['vertical'],['acceleration','gravity'],['down','g']]],['At the top vertical velocity is zero but acceleration remains downward.',[['top','maximum'],['vertical velocity','velocity'],['zero'],['acceleration','gravity']]],['Drag acts opposite to motion and increases with speed in the qualitative model.',[['drag','air resistance'],['opposite','resist'],['speed']]],['Drag reduces horizontal speed and makes the real path shorter/non-symmetric.',[['horizontal'],['speed','velocity'],['reduce','decrease'],['shorter','range','symmetric','steeper']]]],model:'An ideal projectile can be analysed as horizontal and vertical components that share the same time. With no air resistance the horizontal acceleration is zero, so horizontal velocity stays constant. Vertically, acceleration is g downward throughout the flight. At maximum height the vertical velocity is momentarily zero but acceleration is still downward. Air resistance acts opposite the motion and becomes more important as speed rises. It reduces horizontal speed and alters the vertical motion, so the trajectory is shorter and is generally no longer symmetric.'},
 {id:'newton',topic:'Newton’s laws',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain and distinguish',q:'Explain Newton’s three laws and clearly distinguish a balanced pair of forces on one object from a Newton’s third-law pair.',plan:['State the first law in resultant-force form.','State the second law.','Describe a third-law interaction pair and contrast it with balanced forces.'],criteria:[['First law: zero resultant force means constant velocity.',[['zero'],['resultant','net'],['constant velocity','stationary']]],['Second law links resultant force to mass and acceleration.',[['resultant','net'],['force'],['mass'],['acceleration']]],['Uses ΣF = ma or equivalent.',[['ma','f=ma','sumf']]],['Third-law forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.',[['equal'],['opposite'],['force']]],['Third-law pair acts on different objects.',[['different'],['object']]],['Balanced forces act on the same object and sum to zero, unlike a third-law pair.',[['balanced'],['same object','one object'],['zero','resultant']]]],model:'Newton’s first law states that if the resultant force is zero, velocity remains constant, including remaining at rest. Newton’s second law for constant mass is ΣF = ma. Newton’s third law says that interacting objects exert forces on each other that are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. Those two forces act on different objects. Balanced forces, in contrast, act on the same object and have a zero resultant.'},
 {id:'momentum',topic:'Momentum & collisions',marks:6,time:10,command:'Explain and apply',q:'Explain the principle of conservation of momentum and how you would apply it to a one-dimensional collision. Include the condition required for the principle to be valid.',plan:['Define momentum as a vector.','State system conservation.','Explain signs and external impulse condition.'],criteria:[['Momentum is mass multiplied by velocity and is a vector.',[['momentum'],['mass'],['velocity'],['vector','direction']]],['Total momentum before equals total momentum after.',[['momentum'],['before'],['after'],['equal','conserve']]],['Conservation applies to the whole chosen system.',[['system'],['total']]],['External resultant impulse must be negligible/zero during the interaction.',[['external'],['impulse','force'],['negligible','zero','isolated']]],['A positive direction should be chosen and opposite velocities given opposite signs.',[['positive'],['direction'],['sign','negative','opposite']]],['Individual object momenta may change even though total system momentum is conserved.',[['individual','object'],['change'],['total'],['conserve']]]],model:'Momentum is p = mv and is a vector. In an isolated system, or when external resultant impulse is negligible during the collision, total momentum is conserved: total momentum before equals total momentum after. In one dimension choose a positive direction and give velocities signs. Calculate each object’s signed momentum and equate the totals. Individual momenta can change substantially even though the total for the system stays constant.'},
 {id:'impulse',topic:'Impulse & safety',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain',q:'Explain how impulse links force, collision time and change in momentum, and use this to explain how increasing stopping time can reduce injury in a collision.',plan:['Define impulse as change in momentum.','Relate impulse to force–time area.','Apply fixed momentum change to a longer stopping time.'],criteria:[['Impulse equals change in momentum.',[['impulse'],['change'],['momentum']]],['For constant/average force, J = FΔt.',[['force'],['time'],['impulse']]],['For variable force, impulse is area under a force–time graph.',[['area'],['force'],['time'],['graph']]],['For a given initial/final momentum the impulse is fixed.',[['same','fixed','given'],['momentum','impulse']]],['Increasing stopping time reduces average force for the same impulse.',[['increase','longer'],['time'],['reduce','decrease'],['force']]],['Connects lower force to reduced acceleration/stress/injury risk.',[['force'],['injury','acceleration','stress'],['reduce','lower']]]],model:'Impulse is the change in momentum, J = Δp. For a constant or average force, J = FΔt, and for a varying force impulse is the area under the force–time graph. If the same object is brought from the same initial velocity to rest, its momentum change is fixed. Increasing the stopping time therefore reduces the average force. Reducing the force reduces the large accelerations and stresses on the body, helping to reduce injury risk.'},
 {id:'energy',topic:'Work, energy & power',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain and connect',q:'Explain the relationship between work done, energy transfer and power. Include how a force acting at an angle affects the work done.',plan:['Define work as energy transfer.','Use the parallel force component.','Define power as rate of energy transfer.'],criteria:[['Work done is energy transferred by a force through a displacement.',[['work'],['energy'],['transfer'],['displacement']]],['Only the component of force parallel to displacement contributes to W = Fs cosθ.',[['parallel','cos','cosine'],['force'],['displacement']]],['A perpendicular force gives zero work in the simple translational model.',[['perpendicular'],['zero'],['work']]],['Power is rate of energy transfer/work done.',[['power'],['rate'],['energy','work']]],['Uses P = W/t or ΔE/t.',[['time'],['power'],['work','energy']]],['Distinguishes joules for energy from watts for power.',[['joule','j'],['watt','w'],['energy'],['power']]]],model:'Work done is energy transferred when a force acts through a displacement. For a force at angle θ to the displacement, only the parallel component transfers energy, so W = Fs cosθ. A perpendicular force therefore does no work in this simple translational sense. Power is the rate of doing work or transferring energy, P = W/t = ΔE/t. Energy is measured in joules, whereas power is measured in watts.'},
 {id:'springs',topic:'Springs & elastic energy',marks:6,time:9,command:'Explain',q:'Explain Hooke’s law, the limit of proportionality and how the force–extension graph can be used to determine both spring constant and elastic energy.',plan:['State Hooke’s law and its limit.','Interpret gradient.','Interpret area.'],criteria:[['Within the proportional region force is proportional to extension.',[['force'],['proportional'],['extension']]],['Uses F = kΔL and identifies k as spring constant.',[['spring constant','k'],['force'],['extension']]],['Limit of proportionality is where the linear proportional relationship ends.',[['limit'],['proportional'],['linear','straight']]],['Gradient of F against extension is k in the Hookean region.',[['gradient'],['spring constant','k']]],['Area under force–extension graph is elastic energy transferred/stored.',[['area'],['force'],['extension'],['energy']]],['For a linear region the area is ½FΔL = ½k(ΔL)².',[['half','1/2','½'],['energy'],['extension']]]],model:'Within the limit of proportionality, force is proportional to extension, F = kΔL, where k is the spring constant. On a graph of force against extension, the gradient of the initial straight-line region is k. The limit of proportionality is the point beyond which this direct proportionality no longer holds. Elastic energy transferred is the area under the force–extension graph; in the linear Hookean region this triangular area is ½FΔL = ½k(ΔL)².'},
 {id:'materials',topic:'Stress, strain & Young modulus',marks:6,time:10,command:'Explain and compare',q:'Explain the meanings of tensile stress, tensile strain and Young modulus, and describe what a larger Young modulus tells you about a material.',plan:['Define stress.','Define strain.','Define Young modulus and interpret stiffness.'],criteria:[['Stress is force divided by cross-sectional area.',[['stress'],['force'],['area']]],['Stress unit is pascal.',[['stress'],['pa','pascal']]],['Strain is extension divided by original length.',[['strain'],['extension'],['original'],['length']]],['Strain is dimensionless.',[['strain'],['dimensionless','no unit','no units']]],['Young modulus is stress divided by strain in the linear elastic region.',[['young'],['stress'],['strain'],['linear','elastic']]],['Larger Young modulus means a stiffer material/greater stress for the same strain.',[['larger','higher'],['young','modulus'],['stiff','stress']]]],model:'Tensile stress is force divided by original cross-sectional area and is measured in pascals. Tensile strain is extension divided by original length and is dimensionless. In the linear elastic region, Young modulus is stress divided by strain and is the gradient of the stress–strain graph. A larger Young modulus means the material is stiffer: more stress is required to produce the same strain.'},
 {id:'rp3',topic:'Required Practical 3 · g',marks:6,time:10,command:'Describe and evaluate',q:'Describe a method to determine g using free fall. Explain how the data should be processed and identify improvements that reduce uncertainty.',plan:['Describe apparatus and measurements.','Linearise h = ½gt².','Discuss repeats, range and systematic/random error.'],criteria:[['Measure a known fall distance and corresponding fall time.',[['distance','height'],['time']]],['Use electronic timing/light gates/electromagnet timing to reduce reaction-time uncertainty.',[['light gate','electronic','electromagnet','automatic'],['time','timing']]],['Repeat readings and calculate means/identify anomalies.',[['repeat'],['mean','average','anomal']]],['Use h = ½gt² and plot h against t².',[['h','height'],['t^2','t²','time squared'],['plot','graph']]],['Gradient is g/2 so g is twice the gradient.',[['gradient'],['g'],['2','twice','half']]],['Use a wide range/longer times and discuss random or systematic uncertainty.',[['range','longer'],['uncertainty','random','systematic']]]],model:'Measure a known vertical fall distance h and the corresponding time t, preferably with electronic timing such as light gates or an electromagnet release to reduce reaction-time effects. Repeat each measurement and calculate means, checking for anomalies. Take data over a wide range of heights. Since h = ½gt², plot h against t²; the gradient is g/2, so g is twice the gradient. Longer times and a wide range reduce percentage uncertainty, while systematic offsets should be identified separately because repeats do not remove them.'},
 {id:'rp4',topic:'Required Practical 4 · Young modulus',marks:6,time:10,command:'Describe and evaluate',q:'Describe how to determine the Young modulus of a wire experimentally. Include the measurements, data processing and important safety/uncertainty considerations.',plan:['Measure original dimensions accurately.','Measure extension for a range of loads.','Use stress–strain gradient and evaluate uncertainty.'],criteria:[['Measure original wire length.',[['original'],['length']]],['Measure wire diameter with a micrometer at several positions/orientations and find a mean.',[['micrometer'],['diameter'],['repeat','several','mean','average']]],['Calculate cross-sectional area in m².',[['area'],['m2','m²','metre']]],['Apply a range of forces and measure extension.',[['range'],['force','load'],['extension']]],['Calculate stress and strain or plot stress against strain; gradient gives Young modulus.',[['stress'],['strain'],['gradient','young']]],['Keep below damaging loads/secure masses and reduce reading uncertainty with long wire/repeats/appropriate scale.',[['safety','secure','damage','limit'],['uncertainty','repeat','long wire','scale']]]],model:'Measure the original wire length and its diameter with a micrometer at several positions and orientations, then calculate the mean diameter and cross-sectional area in m². Add known loads to apply a range of tensile forces and measure the extension for each load. Calculate stress F/A and strain ΔL/L, then plot stress against strain; the gradient of the initial linear region is Young modulus. Keep the apparatus secure, avoid excessive loads and use repeated measurements and a sufficiently long wire to reduce percentage uncertainty in extension.'}
];

const ER_STORE='mechanics-extended-response-v1';
